{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "03-substitution.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Substitution and capture",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Find the rule for $M[x := N]$, the substitution a β-step does, by repairing the obvious rule where it fails.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A β-step turns $(\\lambda x.\\, M)\\ N$ into \"$M$ with $N$ put in for $x$\". Writing that out looks like a formality: replace every $x$ in $M$ by $N$. It is not, and seeing why is the whole lesson.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Two ways the obvious rule fails",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "First failure: $((\\lambda x.\\, x)\\ x)[x := y]$. Replacing every $x$ gives $(\\lambda y.\\, y)\\ y$. But the $x$ in $\\lambda x.\\, x$ belongs to its own λ (lesson 2); only the last $x$ is free, so the answer is $(\\lambda x.\\, x)\\ y$. Repair: replace only **free** occurrences, and stop at a λ that binds $x$ again. `subst: M, x := N` does it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst: x y, x := λz. z",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst: λx. x y, x := z",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Second failure, subtler. $\\lambda y.\\, x$ ignores its argument and gives $x$. Put $y$ in for $x$ with the repaired rule: $(\\lambda y.\\, x)[x := y] = \\lambda y.\\, y$, the identity.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Predict both before running them. $(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x)\\ y\\ a$ gives $y$ and then $a$ to a function that keeps its first argument. $(\\lambda y.\\, y)\\ a$ is where the step $(\\lambda y.\\, x)[x := y] = \\lambda y.\\, y$ would lead.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. x) y a",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λy. y) a",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The function keeps its first argument, $y$; the naive step led to $a$. The $y$ put in was free, a value from outside, but it landed under a $\\lambda y$ and now means the parameter: it has been **captured**.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> How would you repair it? Lesson 2 has the tool.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The name of a bound variable does not matter, so move it out of the way first. $\\lambda y.\\, x$ is the same function as $\\lambda y'.\\, x$, and $(\\lambda y'.\\, x)[x := y] = \\lambda y'.\\, y$: a function that ignores its argument and gives $y$, as it should. The new name must be **fresh**: free neither in what is put in nor in the body.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Substitution\n> $M[x := N]$ replaces the free occurrences of $x$ in $M$ by $N$:\n> - $x[x := N] = N$, and $y[x := N] = y$ for another variable $y$;\n> - $(M_1\\ M_2)[x := N] = M_1[x := N]\\ M_2[x := N]$;\n> - $(\\lambda x.\\, M)[x := N] = \\lambda x.\\, M$: here $x$ is bound, so there is nothing to replace (the first repair);\n> - $(\\lambda y.\\, M)[x := N] = \\lambda y.\\, M[x := N]$ when $y$ is not free in $N$;\n> - otherwise rename $y$ first, to a fresh $z$: $\\lambda z.\\, M[y := z][x := N]$ (the second).",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The engine does the renaming as its own step:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst: λy. x y, x := y",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst: λy. λx. x y z, z := x y",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A β-step is a substitution, so it renames too:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. x y) y",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The obvious substitution first, with the first repair. It captures:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- Substitution as it first comes to mind: put s for every free x, stopping under a binder named x. -/\ndef substNaive (x : String) (s : Term) : Term → Term\n  | var y => if y = x then s else var y\n  | lam y b => if y = x then lam y b else lam y (substNaive x s b)\n  | app f a => app (substNaive x s f) (substNaive x s a)\n\n-- (λy. x)[x := y] should ignore its argument and return the free y\n#eval (substNaive \"x\" (var \"y\") (lam \"y\" (var \"x\"))).pretty",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem substNaive_self (x : String) (t : Term) : substNaive x (var x) t = t := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Substituting a variable for itself changes nothing. Prove it by induction on the term.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction t with\n  | var y => by_cases h : y = x <;> simp [substNaive, h]\n  | lam y b ih => by_cases h : y = x <;> simp [substNaive, h, ih]\n  | app f a ihf iha => simp [substNaive, ihf, iha]",
      "hints": [
        "`induction t with` gives a case per constructor, with induction hypotheses for the subterms.",
        "In the `var` and `lam` cases, split on `y = x` with `by_cases h : y = x`, then `simp [substNaive, h]` (add `ih` where there is one)."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The real one renames. Renaming $y$ to $z$ is done with the naive substitution, so $z$ must avoid every name in the body, bound ones too: a $\\lambda z$ inside would capture it. And its recursive call is on a renamed body, which is not a subterm, so Lean cannot see on its own that it stops: we say why (renaming keeps the size) and Lean checks it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- A name not in avoid: x, x', x'', … (avoid is finite, so one of the first length + 1 is free). -/\ndef fresh (avoid : List String) (x : String) : String :=\n  go x avoid.length\nwhere\n  go (c : String) : Nat → String\n    | 0 => c\n    | n + 1 => if c ∈ avoid then go (c ++ \"'\") n else c\n\n/-- Every name in a term, bound or free. -/\ndef allVars : Term → List String\n  | var x => [x]\n  | lam x b => x :: allVars b\n  | app f a => allVars f ++ allVars a\n\n/-- Rename the variable y to z. -/\ndef rename (y z : String) (b : Term) : Term := substNaive y (var z) b\n\n/-- Renaming keeps the size: the measure capture-avoiding substitution recurses on. -/\ntheorem size_rename (y z : String) (t : Term) : (rename y z t).size = t.size := by\n  induction t with\n  | var w => by_cases h : w = y <;> simp [rename, substNaive, h, Term.size]\n  | lam w b ih => by_cases h : w = y <;> simp_all [rename, substNaive, Term.size]\n  | app f a ihf iha => simp_all [rename, substNaive, Term.size]\n\n/-- Capture-avoiding substitution t[x := s]. -/\ndef subst (x : String) (s : Term) : Term → Term\n  | var y => if y = x then s else var y\n  | app f a => app (subst x s f) (subst x s a)\n  | lam y b =>\n    if y = x then lam y b\n    else if y ∈ freeVars s then\n      let z := fresh (freeVars s ++ allVars b ++ [x]) y\n      lam z (subst x s (rename y z b))\n    else lam y (subst x s b)\ntermination_by t => t.size\ndecreasing_by all_goals simp_wf <;> simp [size_rename, Term.size] <;> omega\n\n#eval (subst \"x\" (var \"y\") (lam \"y\" (var \"x\"))).pretty\n-- the body binds y', so the fresh name is y''\n#eval (subst \"x\" (var \"y\") (lam \"y\" (lam \"y'\" (app (var \"x\") (var \"y\"))))).pretty",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A `subst` answer is compared with the result up to the names of bound variables, and is not reduced: write the term the substitution gives.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst: x (λx. x y), x := y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute $(x\\ (\\lambda x.\\, x\\ y))[x := y]$.",
      "hints": [
        "Only the first $x$ is free; the λ binds the others."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst: λz. x z, x := z",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute $(\\lambda z.\\, x\\ z)[x := z]$.",
      "hints": [
        "The binder $z$ would capture the $z$ put in: rename it first."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst: λy. x, x := λw. w",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Compute $(\\lambda y.\\, x)[x := \\lambda w.\\, w]$.",
      "hints": [
        "$\\lambda w.\\, w$ has no free variables, so nothing can be captured."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. y x) y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, y\\ x)\\ y$ to normal form, with the renaming you invented.",
      "hints": [
        "The $y$ passed in is free; the $\\lambda y$ would capture it. Rename the binder, say to $z$, first."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Substitution replaces free occurrences only, and renames a binder that would capture a free variable of what is put in. Both rules come from the obvious rule failing, and together they are the whole of β-reduction's work.\n\nWith substitution settled, β-steps can be chained. Does the chain always end, and does it matter which redex goes first? Lesson 4.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
